-- title:
--   Fixed ring of a tangentially diagonal automorphism of uv=π
-- statement:
--   Let $\hat{\mathcal O}$ be a commutative local ring that is complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal, and let $\pi \in \hat{\mathcal O}$ be a non-unit which is a non-zero-divisor and satisfies $\mathfrak m^{k} \subseteq (\pi)$ for some $k \in \mathbb N$. Write $R =$ `UVCrossingModel` $\hat{\mathcal O}\,\pi$ for the quotient of the two-variable power series ring $\mathrm{MvPowerSeries}\,(\mathrm{Fin}\ 2)\,\hat{\mathcal O}$ by the ideal generated by $X_0X_1 - C(\pi)$, and assume $R$ is local and complete for the adic topology of its maximal ideal $\mathfrak m_R$. Let $e \in \mathbb N$ have $e$ invertible in $\hat{\mathcal O}$, and let $\zeta, \zeta' \in \hat{\mathcal O}$ satisfy $\zeta\zeta' = 1$, $\zeta^{e} = 1$, and $\zeta^{m} - 1$ a non-zero-divisor for all $0 < m < e$. Let $\sigma$ be an $\hat{\mathcal O}$-algebra automorphism of $R$ whose $e$-fold iterate fixes every element, and suppose $\sigma(U \pi) - \mathrm{const}\,\pi\,\zeta \cdot U \pi$ and $\sigma(V \pi) - \mathrm{const}\,\pi\,\zeta' \cdot V \pi$ both lie in $\mathfrak m_R^{2}$, where `const` $\pi\,w$ denotes the class of the constant series $C(w)$ and `U`, `V` are the two branch coordinates of the crossing model. Then there exist an $\hat{\mathcal O}$-algebra homomorphism $\iota$ from `UVCrossingModel` $\hat{\mathcal O}\,(\pi^{e})$ to $R$ and a unit $\gamma$ of $R$ such that $\iota$ is injective, an element $z \in R$ satisfies $\sigma z = z$ if and only if $z$ lies in the range of $\iota$, $\gamma - 1 \in \mathfrak m_R$, and $\iota(U(\pi^{e})) = (\gamma \cdot U \pi)^{e}$, $\iota(V(\pi^{e})) = (\gamma^{-1} \cdot V \pi)^{e}$.
--
--   This identifies the fixed ring of an order-$e$ automorphism of the regular crossing $uv = \pi$ that acts diagonally by $(\zeta,\zeta^{-1})$ to first order with a copy of the $A_{e-1}$ crossing $UV = \pi^{e}$, in the coordinate-tracking form that also records the linearising principal unit $\gamma$ and the images of the two branch coordinates. It is the local input for reading off the width of a quotient node, and is cited by the per-node conclusions of the full-level modular curve analysis, including the cases of ramification degree two and three.
